{"theorems":[{"slogan_id":8059737,"theorem_id":23150070,"name":"Theorem 1.2 ({\\bf Weighted Matrix-Tree Theorem})","body":"Let $G$ be a graph with $n$ vertices, and let $\\hat L$ be its weighted Laplacian matrix.\\begin{enumerate} \\item If the eigenvalues of $\\hat L$ are $\\hat\\lambda _0=0,\\hat\\lambda _1,\\dots ,\\hat\\lambda _{n-1}$, then \n\n\\[ \\sum _{T\\in {\\mathcal T}(G)} \\prod _{ij\\in T} e_{ij} = \\frac{\\hat\\lambda _1\\cdots \\hat\\lambda _{n-1}}{n} \\]\n\n where ${\\mathcal T}(G)$ is the set of all spanning trees of $G$. \n\n\\item For $1\\leq i\\leq n$, let $\\hat L_i$ be the \\emph{reduced weighted Laplacian} obtained from $\\hat L$ by deleting the $i^{th}$ row and $i^{th}$ column. Then \n\n\\[ \\sum _{T\\in {\\mathcal T}(G)} \\prod _{ij\\in T} e_{ij} = \\det \\hat L_i. \\]\n\n\\end{enumerate}","slogan":"The weighted Laplacian matrix of a graph has eigenvalues including zero. The product of the nonzero eigenvalues divided by the number of vertices equals the sum over all spanning trees of the product of their edge weights. This sum also equals the determinant of any reduced weighted Laplacian matrix. The reduced matrix is formed by deleting one row and column from the original Laplacian.","theorem_type":"theorem","label":null,"link":null,"paper":{"paper_id":"0802.2576v2","source":"arXiv","title":"Simplicial matrix-tree theorems","authors":["Art M. Duval","Caroline J. Klivans","Jeremy L. Martin"],"link":"http://arxiv.org/abs/0802.2576v2","summary":null,"journal_ref":null,"primary_category":"math.CO","categories":["math.CO"],"citations":0,"year":2008,"journal_published":true},"similarity":0.8440243508830295,"score":0.8440243508830295,"has_metadata":true},{"slogan_id":6023811,"theorem_id":21091970,"name":"Theorem 2.1","body":"Assume $G$ is finite with $n$ vertices. Let\\[  0=\\lambda _1<\\lambda _2\\leq \\cdots \\leq \\lambda _n  \\]be the eigenvalues of $\\Delta $. Then\\[  D(G) = m(G)^{-1} \\frac{\\prod _{e\\in E(G)^\\ast } w(e)}{\\prod _{v\\in V(G)} w(v)} \\prod _{i=2}^n \\lambda _i.  \\]","slogan":"For a finite graph G with n vertices, let the eigenvalues of its Laplacian be ordered from zero to the largest. The number of spanning trees of G equals the product of all nonzero eigenvalues divided by the total vertex weight, multiplied by the ratio of the product of edge weights to the product of vertex weights. This result holds for weighted graphs where weights are assigned to edges and vertices. The formula generalizes Kirchhoff's matrix-tree theorem to the weighted case.","theorem_type":"theorem","label":null,"link":null,"paper":{"paper_id":"1612.07898v1","source":"arXiv","title":"On component groups of Jacobians of quaternionic modular curves","authors":["Mihran Papikian"],"link":"http://arxiv.org/abs/1612.07898v1","summary":null,"journal_ref":null,"primary_category":"math.NT","categories":["math.NT"],"citations":0,"year":2016,"journal_published":true},"similarity":0.8079020862520999,"score":0.8079020862520999,"has_metadata":true}]}